WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:04.042
A shuffle happens when data must be redistributed across workers.

00:00:04.042 --> 00:00:09.342
This often occurs for GROUP BY, ORDER BY, DISTINCT, and many joins.

00:00:09.342 --> 00:00:11.427
Shuffles are not always bad.

00:00:11.427 --> 00:00:17.297
Sometimes they are necessary. But they are important because they involve network and disk work.

00:00:17.297 --> 00:00:22.347
In Spark performance reasoning, always ask where shuffles occur.

00:00:22.347 --> 00:00:27.942
A shuffle moves data across executors so related keys end up together.

00:00:27.942 --> 00:00:33.677
GROUP BY, large joins, and global ORDER BY can trigger expensive shuffles.

00:00:33.677 --> 00:00:40.297
In distributed systems, moving data is often more costly than evaluating the SQL expression itself,

00:00:40.297 --> 00:00:42.201
so plan around data movement.
